  "strategy": "I propose to develop a step function optimization algorithm that focuses on managing peaks in the self-convolution to achieve the objective better than the previous program. My approach is threefold:\n1. Use a gradient-based method to target high-contribution positions\n2. Employ a step-wise optimization procedure\n3. Use aggressive temperature decay in simulated annealing\n\nWe represent the step function by generating a sequence where each element corresponds to a step probability. The objective is minimized by focusing on positions that contribute to the highest convolution values.\n\nHere's the detailed plan:\n\n1. **Initial Validation**\n- Check parent sequence if available\n- Set default length to 4096 if no parent\n- Generate initial uniform sequence\n\n2. **First Optimization Phase**\n- Apply simplex projection to ensure non-negative and proper scale\n- Use log-sum-exp barrier method to compute gradients\n- Iterate with temperature decay until improvement stalls\n\n3. **Refinement Phase**\n- Identify peak positions using moving-window analysis\n- For each peak, perform:\n  - Proximity checking at half-cooling rate\n  - Dynamic coordinate selection along the gradient direction\n  - Power optimization combining linear and exponential adjustments\n\n4. **Local Search Phase**\n- Use two-stage simulated annealing:\n  - Long-range uphill moves with carefully chosen acceptance probability\n  - Short-range moves that maintain structural integrity\n- Cluster neighboring states based on gradient magnitude\n\n5. **Final Stockpiling**\n- Generate clones with slight variations (noise injection)\n- Apply:\n  - High-amplitude probability sweep\n  - Strong temperature gradient\n  - Multiple-peak targeting mechanism\n- Verify each generated sequence against the original objective\n\nThe mathematical justification is maintained by:\n- Operating on the natural logarithmic scale\n- Projecting using simplex projection (entropy preservation)\n- Employing convex combinations during temperature switching\n\nThe algorithm adapts by:\n- Switching temperatures when gradients exceed tolerance\n- Focusing optimization on the most salient peaks\n- Maintaining a history of high-contribution points\n- Dynamically scaling searches between logarithmic space and linear space\n\nWe need a consistent information pathway:\n[Step-wise Optimization Stage]\n    public method:\n        `propose_candidate`\n\n    helper functions available:\n        `evaluate_sequence` (original evaluator)\n        `fast_convolve` (FFT-based convolution)\n        `project_simplex` (probability simplex projection)\n        `compute_subgradient` (convex subgradient computation)\n\n    steps overall:\n        - Initialization\n        -Gradient peniStage\n        -Annealing (steepest descent)\n        -FISHER refinement\n        -Final validation\n\n    budget:\n        2400 seconds\n\n    configuration:\n        - Use 80% budget adaptive adjustment\n        - 20% budget for validation\n        - Focusentirehistory on parent-derived template\n\n    seed/capability:\n        Start from base seed, use informed projections\n\n    adaptive heuristics:\n        -Temperature decay logistically\n        -Move acceptance uniform(0,1)\n        -Gradient smoothing threshold fixed at 0.002\n\n    output format:\n        -final sequence of 4096 non-negative floats\n        -inv\u00e1lido calls are avoided\n        -clustering done with 5 seeds\n\n    safety checks:\n        project_simplex is used only once initially\n        non-negative maintained throughout\n\n    objective:\n        score = 2 * n * max(convolve(a,a))/sum(a)^2\n\n    optimization:\n        min score with constraints\n\n    rationale:\n        The previous program scored 1.514778, so we need a 0.009 improvement.\n\nImplementation details:\n- Use logarithmic transformation to minimize non-convexity\n- Apply \nairement predicted\n admission\u0447\u0435\u0440\u0435\u0437 Aggressive patience\uacfc \u66f4\u591a\u5